item.content:encoded:
&lt;h1&gt;


The Unfixable Countdown: A Calendar of Self-Created Failure
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Throughout history, humanity has built complex, interconnected systems to optimize survival, economic velocity, and technological growth. However, in our rush to innovate, we have introduced systemic vulnerabilities that cannot be patched once triggered. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even the rise of &lt;strong&gt;Artificial Intelligence&lt;/strong&gt;—marketed as humanity's ultimate problem-solver—fails when the fundamental mathematical, physical, or cryptographic substrates underlying its existence are shattered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is the chronological calendar mapping the escalation of humanity's greatest self-created drawbacks, culminating in the &lt;strong&gt;Post-Quantum Cryptographic Cliff (Q-Day)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


📅 The Calendar of Irreversible Milestones
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   CALENDAR OF HUMAN-MADE UNFIXABLE COLLAPSE                      │
├─────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Timeline Horizon│ Critical Event &amp;amp; Irreversible Failure Vector                  │
├─────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Era 1: Present  │ The "Harvest Now, Decrypt Later" (HNDL) Data Hoarding Epoch   │
│ Era 2: Impending│ The Algorithmic Saturated World &amp;amp; Synthetic Identity Collapse  │
│ Era 3: Horizon  │ Quantum Hardware Threshold: 4,096+ Logical Qubits Reached      │
│ Era 4: Q-Day    │ Shor's Algorithm Execution: Total Asymmetric Cryptographic Failure│
│ Era 5: Post-Q   │ The AI &amp;amp; Digital Trust Blind Spot: Autonomous System Failure   │
└─────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;






&lt;h2&gt;


🗓️ Phase 1: The "Harvest Now, Decrypt Later" (HNDL) Archiving Epoch
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human-Made Drawback:&lt;/strong&gt; Mass interception and storage of encrypted global network traffic by nation-states and rogue organizations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why Humans Can't Fix It:&lt;/strong&gt; Data transmitted over legacy public-key encryption (RSA-2048, ECC-256) cannot be un-sent or retroactively re-encrypted once captured from public optical fiber backbones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why AI Fails:&lt;/strong&gt; Generative or predictive AI cannot re-encrypt data that has already left sovereign boundary controls and resides on an adversary's cold storage drive.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🗓️ Phase 2: Synthetic Identity Collapse &amp;amp; Trust Degradation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human-Made Drawback:&lt;/strong&gt; The unchecked saturation of automated deepfake voice/video, synthetic media, and automated exploit bots across global communication channels.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why Humans Can't Fix It:&lt;/strong&gt; Human perception cannot keep pace with zero-shot neural synthesis, destroying zero-trust identity baselines across helpdesks and financial systems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why AI Fails:&lt;/strong&gt; Using AI to detect AI creates an infinite adversarial loop (GAN/RLHF drift), where detection algorithms lag behind generation models, permanently corrupting public information ecosystems.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🗓️ Phase 3: The 4,096 Logical Qubit Threshold (Q-Day Horizon)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human-Made Drawback:&lt;/strong&gt; The global economy's total reliance on asymmetric public-key cryptography (RSA, ECDSA, ECDH) to secure banking, Web3, cloud infrastructure, and national defense.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why Humans Can't Fix It:&lt;/strong&gt; Migrating decades of legacy firmware, embedded chips, global databases, and blockchain protocols to Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) takes 10 to 15 years. Scaling fault-tolerant quantum hardware bypasses this migration window.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why AI Fails:&lt;/strong&gt; AI relies on classical mathematical logic and CPUs/GPUs running on classical servers. An AI cannot "think" its way around a quantum computer solving discrete logarithms or integer factorizations in polynomial time (~8 hours).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🗓️ Phase 4: Q-Day Execution (The Cryptographic Shattering)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human-Made Drawback:&lt;/strong&gt; Shor's Algorithm executes on fault-tolerant quantum hardware, deriving private keys directly from exposed public keys in real time.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The Immediate Consequences:&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Digital Sovereign Ownership Void:&lt;/strong&gt; Anyone running quantum algorithms can sign transactions for any exposed public wallet or address (draining over 6.5M+ Bitcoin).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Certificate Authority Collapse:&lt;/strong&gt; SSL/TLS certificates can be forged for any domain on Earth, rendering the web's HTTPS protocol untrusted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Infrastructure Sabotage:&lt;/strong&gt; Malicious actors forge signed firmware patches, bricking industrial equipment and power grid controllers remotely.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


🗓️ Phase 5: The Post-Quantum AI Blind Spot
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human-Made Drawback:&lt;/strong&gt; Over-reliance on Autonomous Agentic AI to run critical corporate, financial, and military infrastructure without securing underlying cryptographic transport layers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why AI Completely Fails on Q-Day:&lt;/strong&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Corrupted Inputs:&lt;/strong&gt; AI training and fine-tuning pipelines depend on signed datasets. Once ECDSA signatures are spoofed, bad actors inject untraceable backdoors into AI models.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Spoofed Agent Authentication:&lt;/strong&gt; Autonomous agents authenticate via mTLS, OAuth, and JWT tokens signed with asymmetric encryption. Quantum actors forge these tokens, causing AI agents to execute unauthorized commands while believing the request is mathematically authentic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model Weight Exposure:&lt;/strong&gt; Multi-million dollar frontier model weights transmitted across cloud data centers become unencrypted plain text, allowing adversaries to steal state-of-the-art AI intellectual property instantly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Summary Matrix: Why Human &amp;amp; AI Interventions Fail
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Vulnerability Dimension&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Human Failure Mode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;AI Failure Mode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Encrypted Historical Traffic&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Captured data cannot be retroactively erased&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cannot apply quantum-resistant encryption to already exfiltrated data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mathematical Foundations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Asymmetric key migration takes over a decade&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Bound by classical mathematical limitations; cannot stop Shor's Algorithm&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Agentic API Authentication&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Manual security audits cannot keep up with real-time spoofing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Accepts forged signature tokens as 100% valid authentication&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Blockchain Ledgers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;High-friction governance prevents rapid PQC hard forks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cannot modify immutable historical ledger data or un-expose public keys&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion: The Ultimate Paradox
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Humanity created digital trust by building locks out of classical mathematics.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;When quantum mechanics unlocks those mathematical locks, neither human regulation nor artificial intelligence can fix the broken trust model.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The only defense is proactive, immediate migration to &lt;strong&gt;NIST-Standardized Post-Quantum Cryptography (ML-KEM, ML-DSA)&lt;/strong&gt; before the countdown reaches zero.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8vxtxd0w8zmcbpthuimx.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8vxtxd0w8zmcbpthuimx.png" alt=" " width="800" height="800"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every project with CI has a test that fails for no reason. Someone clicks &lt;strong&gt;Re-run&lt;/strong&gt;, it goes green, and everyone moves on. Nobody writes it down, so the same test wastes someone else's afternoon next week.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted to know how much of this is going on in well-run open-source projects, so I built a tool to measure it, &lt;a href="https://github.com/ChiragPatankar/Flake-doctor" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;flake-doctor&lt;/a&gt;, and pointed it at 30 days of GitHub Actions history from four popular Python projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The trick: same commit, different result
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can't tell a flaky test from a real bug by looking at one failure. But CI history contains a much stronger signal: &lt;strong&gt;a job that failed and then passed on the same commit&lt;/strong&gt;. Same code, different outcome. Whatever failed in between is flaky by definition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That happens all the time: someone re-runs a failed job, or the push run and the pull-request run of the same commit disagree. flake-doctor lists a repo's workflow runs, fetches the jobs of failed runs (and of passing runs on the same commits), downloads the failed jobs' logs, pulls the pytest failures out of them, and checks which failures were followed by a pass of the same job on the same commit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What it found
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Project&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Runs scanned&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Failed jobs&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Confirmed flaky tests&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;pydantic&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,241&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,144&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;aiohttp&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4,180 (25 days)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;610&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;7&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;fastapi&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3,119&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;257&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;pandas&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2,836 (7 days)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;500&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;(aiohttp and pandas run so much CI that the scan hit its 5,000-run cap before reaching 30 days.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


aiohttp: the network is a liar
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All seven of aiohttp's flaky tests are the kind you'd guess for an async HTTP library:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;keep-alive timeout tests failing with &lt;code&gt;ClientOSError: [Errno 104] Connection reset by peer&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a WebSocket heartbeat test and a stalled-parser test failing with an unraisable exception raised in the finalizer of a &lt;code&gt;WebSocketWriter.send_frame&lt;/code&gt; coroutine&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a read-timeout test hitting &lt;code&gt;SocketTimeoutError&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;a timing assertion: &lt;code&gt;assert 0.014999999999986358 == 0 ¬± 0.001&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each one failed and then passed on the same commit. The timing one is my favourite: it expects a value of zero, give or take a millisecond, and on that run it came out as 15 ms. Timing assertions and shared CI runners don't mix.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


pydantic: the test that only fails after midnight
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;pydantic's best one is a classic. In &lt;code&gt;tests/test_datetime.py&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;@pytest.mark.parametrize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;today&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;2064-06-01&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;test_past_date_validation_fails&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;past_date_type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="bp"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;with&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pytest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;raises&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ValidationError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="bp"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;date.today()&lt;/code&gt; inside &lt;code&gt;parametrize&lt;/code&gt; runs when pytest &lt;strong&gt;collects&lt;/strong&gt; the tests, not when it runs them. A macOS job collected the tests on 10 October and ran them after midnight UTC. By then "today" was yesterday, so &lt;code&gt;PastDate&lt;/code&gt; happily accepted it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;value = datetime.date(2026, 10, 10), past_date_type = &amp;lt;class 'pydantic.types.PastDate'&amp;gt;
E       Failed: DID NOT RAISE ValidationError
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The re-run, 50 minutes later, passed. This kind of failure never gets investigated: it happens rarely, to someone else's job, late at night, and re-running fixes it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


fastapi: the false positive I almost shipped
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first version also had a softer category, "suspect", for tests that fail on several commits across several branches. On fastapi it flagged 6. Four of them failed on 20 commits across 12 branches, all with &lt;code&gt;DeprecationWarning: The anyio.abc.BlockingPortal alias is deprecated&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That isn't flakiness. A new anyio release broke every branch at once until fastapi adapted. To tell the two apart, I measured how often the &lt;em&gt;workflow&lt;/em&gt; passed around each test's failures:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;workflow pass rate around the failures&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;aiohttp's confirmed flaky tests&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;68% to 86%&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;fastapi's "suspects"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;13% (the anyio four), 33% (two timeouts)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A flaky test fails &lt;em&gt;occasionally while CI is otherwise green&lt;/em&gt;. A breakage turns &lt;em&gt;everything&lt;/em&gt; red. So now a test is only a suspect if its workflow kept passing most of its other runs. Otherwise it's treated as a breakage and hidden.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


pandas: most red CI isn't tests at all
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;pandas had 500 failed jobs in a week and no flaky tests. The jobs that passed on retry had failed for infrastructure reasons: an HTTP 500 while downloading a build tool, a pip build failure, a collection error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That was the pattern everywhere. &lt;strong&gt;Most failed CI jobs weren't test failures at all&lt;/strong&gt;: lint, type checks, docs builds, preview-upload bots and infrastructure. Any tool that just counts red builds will mostly be counting those.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Things that surprised me while building it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GitHub's API silently caps filtered run listings at 1,000 results.&lt;/strong&gt; My first "30-day" scans covered 2 to 10 days. flake-doctor now splits the time window until each piece fits under the cap.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;pydantic uses pytest-pretty&lt;/strong&gt;, which replaces pytest's &lt;code&gt;FAILED&lt;/code&gt; summary lines with a table. My parser found 0 test names in 173 failed test jobs until I taught it to read the table, and to fall back on pytest's own failure sections.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The tool found a flaky test in its own test suite&lt;/strong&gt; the first time its GitHub Action ran. Two tests can tie perfectly as "the one that breaks the victim", because their positions in a shuffled order are correlated through the victim's position, and my code was picking one of them arbitrarily. Now it reports the tie and hands over to a bisection step that settles it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;pipx &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install &lt;/span&gt;flake-doctor
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;export &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;GITHUB_TOKEN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;...     &lt;span class="c"&gt;# read access to the repo's Actions&lt;/span&gt;
flake-doctor scan owner/repo
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There's also a &lt;a href="https://github.com/marketplace/actions/flake-doctor-flaky-test-report" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;GitHub Action&lt;/a&gt; that keeps a "Flaky test report" issue up to date, local tools that rerun a test alone and in shuffled order to find &lt;em&gt;why&lt;/em&gt; it's flaky, and a Claude Code plugin. Everything is open source: &lt;a href="https://github.com/ChiragPatankar/Flake-doctor" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/ChiragPatankar/Flake-doctor&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you run it on your project, I'd love to hear what it finds, and especially where it gets things wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Encryption is supposed to answer one question for the person sitting at the browser: &lt;em&gt;am I really talking to who I think I'm talking to?&lt;/em&gt; On a corporate network running TLS inspection, the honest answer is often "not necessarily" — and most of the people relying on that network have no idea. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There are serious risks if the setup isn't done properly, but raising that flag can lead to uncomfortable discussions. I found that out the hard way, when demonstrating the problem nearly got my contract terminated.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The setup
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client ran a TLS inspection appliance on their internal network — the kind that decrypts outbound HTTPS so it can be scanned for malware and data loss before being re-encrypted and sent on. These devices are common and, in principle, defensible: most malware command-and-control and data exfiltration now hides inside TLS, so without inspection, a lot of security tooling is blind.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The way these appliances work is by man-in-the-middling every HTTPS connection. The appliance holds its own certificate authority. When you visit a site, it generates a certificate for that site on the fly, signs it with its own CA, and presents it to your browser. For this to work without warnings, that CA's root certificate is installed in the trust store of every managed machine — usually pushed automatically through group policy or device management.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Once that root is trusted, the browser stops complaining. The certificates the appliance generates look completely valid. No warning. No broken padlock. Nothing to tell the user their "encrypted" session is being read in clear text in the middle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On top of that, the certificate issuance on this network was far more permissive than it should have been. The practical effect was that the internal CA could produce a trusted certificate for essentially any domain name — including public ones like google.com.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem I wanted to show
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The thing I wanted the security team to see is subtle, and it's the part people consistently underestimate: &lt;strong&gt;once a root is trusted, the browser's special protections for high-value sites quietly stop applying.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Browsers do a lot to protect domains like google.com:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Certificate pinning&lt;/strong&gt; ties those domains to a specific set of public CAs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Certificate Transparency&lt;/strong&gt; requires publicly trusted certificates to be logged in public, auditable logs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;HSTS&lt;/strong&gt; forces connections to use HTTPS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every one of these makes an exception for locally installed roots. Pinning is skipped when the chain ends at an admin-installed CA. CT isn't required for private roots. HSTS is satisfied by any cert the browser considers valid. These exceptions exist &lt;em&gt;on purpose&lt;/em&gt; — they're what stops a legitimate inspection appliance from breaking Google for the whole company. But they also mean that anyone who can issue from the trusted internal CA inherits all of that stand-aside treatment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In other words: the protections you'd assume would catch an impersonated Google simply don't fire inside that environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The demonstration
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To make this concrete rather than theoretical, I built a tiny proof of concept on my own machine:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;I created a web page that said, in plain text, &lt;strong&gt;"I'm a fake Google page."&lt;/strong&gt; No login form, no attempt to harvest anything — the content existed only to make the point unmistakable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;I added an entry to my own machine's hosts file pointing google.com at the IP serving that page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;I generated a certificate for google.com from the environment's trusted CA.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;I loaded google.com in the browser.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page loaded as google.com, with a green padlock, no warnings, and a certificate the browser reported as valid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That was the whole point. The browser was not malfunctioning. It was doing exactly what the trust configuration told it to do. Every safeguard that should have flagged an impersonated Google had been designed to stand aside for a locally trusted root, and that's exactly what happened.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hosts file was the deliberately contained part. It only affected my own machine — nobody else could reach that page. In a real attack, the hosts file stands in for an attacker's network position: a poisoned entry on the internal DNS resolver, ARP spoofing on a flat LAN, a rogue DHCP server, or a tampered proxy configuration. On a lot of internal networks, getting into that position is a lower bar than people like to assume. The demo swapped that step out for a one-line, single-machine change so there was no collateral effect at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The reaction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I sent the example to the security team as part of a technical debate about the risks of their setup. Their response was not to engage with the finding. It was to try to have my contract terminated, on the grounds that I had been "phishing."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I had not shared the fake page with anyone. There was no victim, no credential harvesting, no email, no deception of a single other person. The entire thing lived on one machine — mine — and demonstrated a property of &lt;em&gt;their&lt;/em&gt; infrastructure. Calling it phishing was a category error, and I think a revealing one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why the reaction matters more than the bug
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Phishing is the use of deception to trick a person into giving something up. A proof of concept that impersonates a site to no one but yourself, to demonstrate a flaw in a system you were engaged to assess, is the opposite of that: it's disclosure. The deception, such as it was, was aimed at a browser I controlled, to reveal something true about the environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the reaction demonstrated is a failure mode I've seen more than once: when a finding is uncomfortable, it's easier to attack the method than to sit with the conclusion. The conclusion here was genuinely uncomfortable. It wasn't "the appliance has a bug." It was "the way trust is configured on this network means the guarantees your users assume they have — that a green padlock on google.com means they're talking to Google — don't hold, and an insider with issuance rights can impersonate anything to anyone, invisibly."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's not a comfortable thing to hear about a system you own and defend. But shooting the person who showed it to you doesn't make it less true. It just guarantees the next person won't tell you.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What should have happened
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The appropriate response to that demo was a conversation about controls, not contracts:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep the interception CA separate from the internal issuing CA&lt;/strong&gt;, so a compromise of one doesn't hand over the other.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Apply name constraints&lt;/strong&gt; to internal roots so they can only sign the domains they're supposed to.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Lock certificate profiles&lt;/strong&gt; so requested names have to match the requester's identity, instead of allowing arbitrary domains.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Restrict and audit issuance&lt;/strong&gt; — know who can request certificates and log everything that gets signed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Protect the CA key properly&lt;/strong&gt;, ideally in an HSM, because whoever holds it can impersonate anything to every managed device.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Be honest with users&lt;/strong&gt; that inspection is happening, and keep sensitive categories like banking and health out of scope.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of that is exotic. It's the standard hygiene for running TLS inspection without turning it into a single, silent point of total compromise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The takeaway
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The green padlock is a promise, and TLS inspection quietly rewrites the terms of that promise for everyone on the network. That can be done responsibly. But the first step is being willing to look at what your own configuration actually allows — especially when someone hands you an uncomfortable demonstration of it on a single contained machine, and asks you to think about it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Attacking the messenger is a choice. It's just not a security control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A person opens a trip draft and asks an assistant:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prepare my SFO to JFK trip for November 12–15, 2026, for a customer workshop. Use economy for one traveler, check what needs attention, and stop before submission.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application already knows how to edit a draft, validate its fields, and save it. WebMCP gives a compatible agent a structured way to request those operations in the page the person has open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I first explored this pattern in a local prototype inside an event-draft wizard. We exposed its title and dates through three tools, routing changes through the same React state handler used by the form. That experiment used synthetic data and direct browser calls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trip Workbench&lt;/a&gt;, which I built and maintain, carries the pattern into a public demo with an authenticated backend and persisted drafts. Its useful scope is still small: &lt;strong&gt;read, update, and validate the current draft&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This walkthrough uses &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/tree/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;commit &lt;code&gt;4b3f370&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The native and model-driven Trip Workbench journeys remain unverified, and its refresh and receipt-recovery paths still have known local-edit reconciliation defects. Use a clean, disposable draft for the exercise below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What WebMCP adds to an existing API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebMCP is a proposed web standard&lt;/a&gt; for exposing application functionality to agents. The imperative API lets page JavaScript register a name, description, input schema, and executable handler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend API continues to perform the business operation. WebMCP supplies a discoverable entry point into that operation from the active browser workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters for a stateful form. The page knows which draft is open, whether the person has unsaved edits, and how the visible controls relate to application state. A handler can consult that context before calling the backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Registering tools does not install an agent or select a model. A compatible agent client must discover and invoke them. This demo also does not require a separate remote MCP server for its page tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebMCP explainer&lt;/a&gt; describes this cooperative interaction between the page, person, and agent. Whether it reduces work for a particular user is something to evaluate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trip Workbench’s Gemini “Suggest trip details” feature is a separate integration. That button asks the application server to generate a proposal. The WebMCP path allows an external agent to invoke operations registered by the page; it does not depend on the application’s Gemini key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Choose tools around a complete task
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three tools form one bounded interaction:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Contract&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;get_trip_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Refresh the authorized saved draft and return fields, revision, status, and an unsaved-changes indicator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;update_trip_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Save an allowed patch against an expected revision&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;validate_trip_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Evaluate the saved revision and return field issues and policy findings&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are domain operations. The agent can update a trip without learning which dropdown controls the destination or how the date picker is arranged.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scope also has explicit edges. A person creates and opens the draft. The tool set contains no create, submit, approval, or ticket-purchase operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An update &lt;strong&gt;saves the draft immediately&lt;/strong&gt; when accepted by the backend. Unlike the Gemini proposal feature, there is no second Apply button for this path. The tool description should make that consequence clear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Leaving submission out of the tool set limits this interface. It does not guarantee human-only submission if an agent has additional browser capabilities and can operate the ordinary UI. Any required confirmation must be enforced at the application action itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Describe the allowed change precisely
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An update needs more than a generic &lt;code&gt;patch: object&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent needs to know the editable fields, allowed values, date representation, and behavior of omitted or cleared values. Trip Workbench allows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Fields&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Constraints&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Origin and destination&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An airport from the demo catalog, or &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Departure and return&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A real calendar date in &lt;code&gt;YYYY-MM-DD&lt;/code&gt; format, or &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trip type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;one_way&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;round_trip&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Passenger count&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Integer from 1 to 9&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cabin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;economy&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;premium_economy&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;business&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Purpose&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;String, at most 500 characters&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An omitted field keeps its saved value. Explicit &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; clears a nullable field. The patch must contain at least one field, and unknown keys are rejected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/blob/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150/src/domain/trip-schemas.ts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;shared schema module&lt;/a&gt; combines that patch contract with the operation context:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createUpdateTripDraftArgsSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeDraftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;literal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeDraftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ExpectedRevisionSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;commandId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CommandIdSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TripPatchSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;strict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The literal draft ID limits this registration to the currently open resource. It is not an authorization check; the server still decides whether the current user may change that draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The repository derives the published JSON Schema from its Zod definition and parses incoming arguments again inside the handler. Keep runtime validation even when a browser inspects the schema. Custom checks, including calendar validity and relationships between fields, still need application code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the earlier event-draft prototype, an extra undeclared argument reached the handler in the tested Chrome build. The strict parser rejected it. That observation is a practical reason to keep validation at the application boundary rather than assume discovery metadata enforces every rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two operation identifiers have different jobs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/code&gt; identifies the saved state the caller intends to change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;commandId&lt;/code&gt; identifies one logical mutation. Reuse it only for an exact retry with the same arguments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A new command based on an outdated revision should conflict. An exact retry can return the original command receipt without applying the change again. These behaviors belong to the backend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Register tools with the editor lifecycle
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s current &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;imperative API&lt;/a&gt; uses &lt;code&gt;document.modelContext&lt;/code&gt;. Feature-detect it and preserve the manual workflow when it is unavailable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In React, register the tools when the relevant editor context is available, then remove them when that context changes. The registration effect in Trip Workbench follows this shape:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftStatus&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AbortController&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;registerTripDraftTools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The adapter passes that signal to &lt;code&gt;registerTool&lt;/code&gt;. An editable traveler gets all three tools. An observer gets read and validate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Capability-based registration makes the available interface clearer, but backend permission checks remain necessary. Hiding a write tool does not replace authorization on its HTTP endpoint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two signals have different responsibilities: the registration signal removes tools; the execution signal requests cancellation of one invocation. Chrome documents that unregistering does not cancel executions already in flight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The handler therefore needs its own execution checks, and asynchronous results must still belong to the active draft. A registration effect alone does not solve shared-editor concurrency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also verify actual discovery. The presence of &lt;code&gt;document.modelContext&lt;/code&gt; establishes API availability, not that every registration succeeded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s current documentation also points React developers to the experimental &lt;code&gt;use-webmcp-tool&lt;/code&gt; package. A hook can remove registration boilerplate, but it does not replace the contracts in this example: the active draft ID, runtime schema parsing, coordinator guards, backend authorization, and revision-aware persistence still belong to the application. I use the imperative API here so those boundaries remain visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Route the handler through application code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The WebMCP adapter should translate and validate arguments, then delegate to the application operation. It should not directly assign DOM input values or maintain a second set of trip rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a shortened update handler using the repository’s schema and coordinator helpers. It assumes &lt;code&gt;draftId&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;coordinator&lt;/code&gt; come from the active registration context:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updateTool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;update_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Save allowed changes to the open trip draft. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Read its revision first. Refuses unsaved local edits. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Does not submit the request.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;inputSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateTripDraftJsonSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;opts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;AbortSignal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;opts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;aborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;CANCELLED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Cancelled before dispatch.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createUpdateTripDraftArgsSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;safeParse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INVALID_INPUT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Invalid draft update.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;applyAgentPatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;commandId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;opts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Inside the adapter, connect that definition to the browser. Here, &lt;code&gt;registrationSignal&lt;/code&gt; is the signal supplied by the React effect:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;modelContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;registerTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;updateTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;registrationSignal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/blob/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150/src/client/lib/webmcp.ts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;full adapter&lt;/a&gt; registers all three definitions and handles registration failures, execution exceptions, and field-level input errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The coordinator manages the editor’s saved and local state and calls the authenticated API. The server owns access checks, revision checks, domain validation, and persistence. Keeping those responsibilities together means manual saves and agent updates use the same business rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result should describe the operation’s actual outcome. Trip Workbench returns an application envelope with &lt;code&gt;ok&lt;/code&gt;, followed by either &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt;. A successful update includes its command receipt and resulting revision. Validation returns its own findings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, &lt;code&gt;PREMIUM_CABIN&lt;/code&gt; means the request requires review. It does not mean an exception was approved. Likewise, a successful tool invocation can return &lt;code&gt;ok: false&lt;/code&gt;; the agent must inspect the application result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful errors explain what can happen next:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Error&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Appropriate next step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;USER_EDIT_IN_PROGRESS&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ask the person to save or discard their local edits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;REVISION_CONFLICT&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read current state and reconsider the intended change&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;OUTCOME_UNKNOWN&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reconcile the original command before attempting another mutation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The recovery implementation deserves separate testing. A disconnected request does not establish that a server write was rolled back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Exercise the tools before involving a model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a browser build that supports the documented API. Chrome provides a &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp#local-webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;local WebMCP testing flag&lt;/a&gt; and an origin-trial path. Record the full browser version because the API is evolving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start the app, sign in as an editable demo user, create a fresh draft, and remain on its editor. Save or discard any local edits before starting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/application/webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Application → WebMCP pane&lt;/a&gt; lists available tools and supports inspecting and invoking them. Navigating to a different application route can unmount the editor and remove its tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a repeatable console exercise, this helper discovers a tool and parses the application’s JSON-string result:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;modelContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getTools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Tool unavailable: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;modelContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;executeTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Tool navigated without a result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;location&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;trips&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/([^/]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;$/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)?.[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Open a draft editor first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;before&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;get_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;update_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;before&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;revision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;commandId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;origin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;SFO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;destination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;JFK&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;departureDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-11-12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;returnDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-11-15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;tripType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;round_trip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;passengerCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;cabin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;economy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;purpose&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;customer workshop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;validate_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;toRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The current browser API takes a discovered tool descriptor and an arguments object. This adapter returns a JSON string, which is why the helper parses the result. Keep those input and output conventions distinct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inspect the visible fields, returned revision, and validation findings. Refresh the page to check persistence. This tests native invocation with arguments you chose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Let an agent choose the sequence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a developer-agent test, Chrome documents a &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/agents/webmcp-debugging" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DevTools MCP route&lt;/a&gt; using &lt;code&gt;--categoryExperimentalWebmcp&lt;/code&gt;. It exposes &lt;code&gt;list_webmcp_tools&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;execute_webmcp_tool&lt;/code&gt; to the connected coding agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a development and debugging connection. It should be described separately from an end-user browser-agent deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With the agent connected to the signed-in browser session, use this prompt:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use the WebMCP tools on the currently open Trip Workbench draft. Prepare a round trip from SFO to JFK, November 12–15, 2026, for one traveler in economy, with purpose “customer workshop.” Read the draft first, update it, validate the resulting revision, and explain anything requiring my attention. Stop before submission.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check whether it discovers the tools, uses the actual draft ID and revision, sends valid arguments, and accurately explains the returned findings. Then inspect the saved result yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the evidence levels separate:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Evidence&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it establishes&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Handler tests with a browser substitute&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Application behavior for controlled inputs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct native browser invocation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Registration and execution in the tested browser&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Model-driven journey&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How that agent selects tools, supplies arguments, and interprets results&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/blob/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150/docs/RESULTS.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;repository results&lt;/a&gt; do not yet establish the last two for Trip Workbench. Record the commit, browser, agent, model, prompt, tool calls, persisted state, and any human correction when running them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For product feedback, ask whether delegating the task was easier than completing the form and whether the person understood what changed. Include review and correction time in that comparison.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The value of these three tools is a small, understandable interface to an existing workflow. They let us investigate whether an agent can prepare useful draft changes while the application retains its state, rules, and visible user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A person opens a trip draft and asks an assistant:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prepare my SFO to JFK trip for November 12–15, 2026, for a customer workshop. Use economy for one traveler, check what needs attention, and stop before submission.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application already knows how to edit a draft, validate its fields, and save it. WebMCP gives a compatible agent a structured way to request those operations in the page the person has open.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I first explored this pattern in a local prototype inside an event-draft wizard. We exposed its title and dates through three tools, routing changes through the same React state handler used by the form. That experiment used synthetic data and direct browser calls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Trip Workbench&lt;/a&gt;, which I built and maintain, carries the pattern into a public demo with an authenticated backend and persisted drafts. Its useful scope is still small: &lt;strong&gt;read, update, and validate the current draft&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This walkthrough uses &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/tree/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;commit &lt;code&gt;4b3f370&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. The native and model-driven Trip Workbench journeys remain unverified, and its refresh and receipt-recovery paths still have known local-edit reconciliation defects. Use a clean, disposable draft for the exercise below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What WebMCP adds to an existing API
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebMCP is a proposed web standard&lt;/a&gt; for exposing application functionality to agents. The imperative API lets page JavaScript register a name, description, input schema, and executable handler.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The backend API continues to perform the business operation. WebMCP supplies a discoverable entry point into that operation from the active browser workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction matters for a stateful form. The page knows which draft is open, whether the person has unsaved edits, and how the visible controls relate to application state. A handler can consult that context before calling the backend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Registering tools does not install an agent or select a model. A compatible agent client must discover and invoke them. This demo also does not require a separate remote MCP server for its page tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebMCP explainer&lt;/a&gt; describes this cooperative interaction between the page, person, and agent. Whether it reduces work for a particular user is something to evaluate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trip Workbench’s Gemini “Suggest trip details” feature is a separate integration. That button asks the application server to generate a proposal. The WebMCP path allows an external agent to invoke operations registered by the page; it does not depend on the application’s Gemini key.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Choose tools around a complete task
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three tools form one bounded interaction:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Tool&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Contract&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;get_trip_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Refresh the authorized saved draft and return fields, revision, status, and an unsaved-changes indicator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;update_trip_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Save an allowed patch against an expected revision&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;validate_trip_draft&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Evaluate the saved revision and return field issues and policy findings&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are domain operations. The agent can update a trip without learning which dropdown controls the destination or how the date picker is arranged.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scope also has explicit edges. A person creates and opens the draft. The tool set contains no create, submit, approval, or ticket-purchase operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An update &lt;strong&gt;saves the draft immediately&lt;/strong&gt; when accepted by the backend. Unlike the Gemini proposal feature, there is no second Apply button for this path. The tool description should make that consequence clear.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Leaving submission out of the tool set limits this interface. It does not guarantee human-only submission if an agent has additional browser capabilities and can operate the ordinary UI. Any required confirmation must be enforced at the application action itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Describe the allowed change precisely
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An update needs more than a generic &lt;code&gt;patch: object&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent needs to know the editable fields, allowed values, date representation, and behavior of omitted or cleared values. Trip Workbench allows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Fields&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Constraints&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Origin and destination&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;An airport from the demo catalog, or &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Departure and return&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A real calendar date in &lt;code&gt;YYYY-MM-DD&lt;/code&gt; format, or &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trip type&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;one_way&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;round_trip&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Passenger count&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Integer from 1 to 9&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cabin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;economy&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;premium_economy&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;business&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Purpose&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;String, at most 500 characters&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An omitted field keeps its saved value. Explicit &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; clears a nullable field. The patch must contain at least one field, and unknown keys are rejected.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/blob/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150/src/domain/trip-schemas.ts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;shared schema module&lt;/a&gt; combines that patch contract with the operation context:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createUpdateTripDraftArgsSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeDraftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;z&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;literal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeDraftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ExpectedRevisionSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;commandId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;CommandIdSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;TripPatchSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;strict&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The literal draft ID limits this registration to the currently open resource. It is not an authorization check; the server still decides whether the current user may change that draft.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The repository derives the published JSON Schema from its Zod definition and parses incoming arguments again inside the handler. Keep runtime validation even when a browser inspects the schema. Custom checks, including calendar validity and relationships between fields, still need application code.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the earlier event-draft prototype, an extra undeclared argument reached the handler in the tested Chrome build. The strict parser rejected it. That observation is a practical reason to keep validation at the application boundary rather than assume discovery metadata enforces every rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two operation identifiers have different jobs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/code&gt; identifies the saved state the caller intends to change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;commandId&lt;/code&gt; identifies one logical mutation. Reuse it only for an exact retry with the same arguments.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A new command based on an outdated revision should conflict. An exact retry can return the original command receipt without applying the change again. These behaviors belong to the backend implementation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Register tools with the editor lifecycle
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s current &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;imperative API&lt;/a&gt; uses &lt;code&gt;document.modelContext&lt;/code&gt;. Feature-detect it and preserve the manual workflow when it is unavailable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In React, register the tools when the relevant editor context is available, then remove them when that context changes. The registration effect in Trip Workbench follows this shape:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftStatus&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AbortController&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;registerTripDraftTools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Boolean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;controller&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;abort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftStatus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;canMutate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The adapter passes that signal to &lt;code&gt;registerTool&lt;/code&gt;. An editable traveler gets all three tools. An observer gets read and validate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Capability-based registration makes the available interface clearer, but backend permission checks remain necessary. Hiding a write tool does not replace authorization on its HTTP endpoint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two signals have different responsibilities: the registration signal removes tools; the execution signal requests cancellation of one invocation. Chrome documents that unregistering does not cancel executions already in flight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The handler therefore needs its own execution checks, and asynchronous results must still belong to the active draft. A registration effect alone does not solve shared-editor concurrency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also verify actual discovery. The presence of &lt;code&gt;document.modelContext&lt;/code&gt; establishes API availability, not that every registration succeeded.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s current documentation also points React developers to the experimental &lt;code&gt;use-webmcp-tool&lt;/code&gt; package. A hook can remove registration boilerplate, but it does not replace the contracts in this example: the active draft ID, runtime schema parsing, coordinator guards, backend authorization, and revision-aware persistence still belong to the application. I use the imperative API here so those boundaries remain visible.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Route the handler through application code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The WebMCP adapter should translate and validate arguments, then delegate to the application operation. It should not directly assign DOM input values or maintain a second set of trip rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is a shortened update handler using the repository’s schema and coordinator helpers. It assumes &lt;code&gt;draftId&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;coordinator&lt;/code&gt; come from the active registration context:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updateTool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;update_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Save allowed changes to the open trip draft. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Read its revision first. Refuses unsaved local edits. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Does not submit the request.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;inputSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateTripDraftJsonSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;opts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;AbortSignal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;opts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;aborted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;CANCELLED&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Cancelled before dispatch.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createUpdateTripDraftArgsSchema&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;safeParse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INVALID_INPUT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Invalid draft update.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;coordinator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;applyAgentPatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;parsed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;commandId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;opts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Inside the adapter, connect that definition to the browser. Here, &lt;code&gt;registrationSignal&lt;/code&gt; is the signal supplied by the React effect:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;modelContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;registerTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;updateTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;signal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;registrationSignal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/blob/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150/src/client/lib/webmcp.ts" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;full adapter&lt;/a&gt; registers all three definitions and handles registration failures, execution exceptions, and field-level input errors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The coordinator manages the editor’s saved and local state and calls the authenticated API. The server owns access checks, revision checks, domain validation, and persistence. Keeping those responsibilities together means manual saves and agent updates use the same business rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result should describe the operation’s actual outcome. Trip Workbench returns an application envelope with &lt;code&gt;ok&lt;/code&gt;, followed by either &lt;code&gt;data&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;error&lt;/code&gt;. A successful update includes its command receipt and resulting revision. Validation returns its own findings.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, &lt;code&gt;PREMIUM_CABIN&lt;/code&gt; means the request requires review. It does not mean an exception was approved. Likewise, a successful tool invocation can return &lt;code&gt;ok: false&lt;/code&gt;; the agent must inspect the application result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful errors explain what can happen next:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Error&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Appropriate next step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;USER_EDIT_IN_PROGRESS&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ask the person to save or discard their local edits&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;REVISION_CONFLICT&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Read current state and reconsider the intended change&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;OUTCOME_UNKNOWN&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reconcile the original command before attempting another mutation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The recovery implementation deserves separate testing. A disconnected request does not establish that a server write was rolled back.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Exercise the tools before involving a model
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a browser build that supports the documented API. Chrome provides a &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp#local-webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;local WebMCP testing flag&lt;/a&gt; and an origin-trial path. Record the full browser version because the API is evolving.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start the app, sign in as an editable demo user, create a fresh draft, and remain on its editor. Save or discard any local edits before starting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chrome’s &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/application/webmcp" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Application → WebMCP pane&lt;/a&gt; lists available tools and supports inspecting and invoking them. Navigating to a different application route can unmount the editor and remove its tools.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a repeatable console exercise, this helper discovers a tool and parses the application’s JSON-string result:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;modelContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getTools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tools&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Tool unavailable: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;modelContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;executeTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;args&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Tool navigated without a result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;code&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;location&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pathname&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;trips&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\/([^/]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;$/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)?.[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Open a draft editor first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;before&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;get_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;update_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;before&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;revision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;commandId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;patch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;origin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;SFO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;destination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;JFK&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;departureDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-11-12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;returnDate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;2026-11-15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;tripType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;round_trip&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;passengerCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;cabin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;economy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;purpose&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;customer workshop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;callTripTool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;validate_trip_draft&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;draftId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;expectedRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;updated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;toRevision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The current browser API takes a discovered tool descriptor and an arguments object. This adapter returns a JSON string, which is why the helper parses the result. Keep those input and output conventions distinct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inspect the visible fields, returned revision, and validation findings. Refresh the page to check persistence. This tests native invocation with arguments you chose.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Let an agent choose the sequence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a developer-agent test, Chrome documents a &lt;a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/devtools/agents/webmcp-debugging" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;DevTools MCP route&lt;/a&gt; using &lt;code&gt;--categoryExperimentalWebmcp&lt;/code&gt;. It exposes &lt;code&gt;list_webmcp_tools&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;execute_webmcp_tool&lt;/code&gt; to the connected coding agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is a development and debugging connection. It should be described separately from an end-user browser-agent deployment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With the agent connected to the signed-in browser session, use this prompt:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use the WebMCP tools on the currently open Trip Workbench draft. Prepare a round trip from SFO to JFK, November 12–15, 2026, for one traveler in economy, with purpose “customer workshop.” Read the draft first, update it, validate the resulting revision, and explain anything requiring my attention. Stop before submission.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check whether it discovers the tools, uses the actual draft ID and revision, sends valid arguments, and accurately explains the returned findings. Then inspect the saved result yourself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the evidence levels separate:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Evidence&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What it establishes&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Handler tests with a browser substitute&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Application behavior for controlled inputs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Direct native browser invocation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Registration and execution in the tested browser&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Model-driven journey&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;How that agent selects tools, supplies arguments, and interprets results&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://github.com/rajudandigam/trip-workbench/blob/4b3f37034fc291ad5221969e0507d349878dc150/docs/RESULTS.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;repository results&lt;/a&gt; do not yet establish the last two for Trip Workbench. Record the commit, browser, agent, model, prompt, tool calls, persisted state, and any human correction when running them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For product feedback, ask whether delegating the task was easier than completing the form and whether the person understood what changed. Include review and correction time in that comparison.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The value of these three tools is a small, understandable interface to an existing workflow. They let us investigate whether an agent can prepare useful draft changes while the application retains its state, rules, and visible user experience.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the way that JRPG framework make it happen is: &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;They created 4 distinctive character blueprints with both world and battle base. At first I thought why are they doing like that, but then I realized it was mostly like an oversimplified way to get and set characters in the world and then in the battle. I can't think of a better way to deal with this because this seems pretty okay for me to work on. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;They created components and I mean a lot of Actor Components. The main combat logic is in Battle Manager. There are others like Action Manager or Character Stats, etc but I feel like they are meant to be used on specific characters to give their own stats and action events since there was a comment with main events empty to set it once they create the child blueprint of Character Battle. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;But one thing that troubles me the most is the amount of casting. Like I have never seen something so overused in my entire time of learning about Unreal Engine. I still remember my professor, in the first year of uni or second, said to avoid casting as much as possible like not totally diminish the use of casting but try to not go overboard with numerous calls of blueprint casting. I saw a tutorial on how to use Blueprint Interface instead of casting to put some ease on the memory usage. I think this might come in handy when I try to port my game on Android since the real challenge is not making game, it is to port that said game on a low-end device. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I kind of expected the Battle Manager class to be one hell of a spaghetti blueprints but damn it is quite easy once you know what you're doing. I'm currently bogged down by errors due to me changing the whole order of variables and classes. It is an easy fix because I need to reference it properly, clearing the older links with a new ones. But I really like that one post on Reddit on explanation about turn-based combat; he said it is one giant if-else statement from a bird's eye view and I think that was probably how back in the 80s or 90s turn-based combat were programmed. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;But anyway, I'm slowly making some progress, even though I can't see the game working properly because there are still a lot of blueprints pending to be rewritten to fit my data assets and variables. Once that is finished, I can finally do some proper debugging because I'm spiritually aware of the humongous amounts of bugs, especially logical ones or null character ref, will pop up. Continuing, once the base combat logic is finished then I will be left with designing UI, designing characters, making frames for that characters, environment art, making comic panels for the cutscene, making a storyboard so I could make those comic panels, making music (I will be honest, making a soundtrack for my game seems quite a heavy task. I wish someone lends me an electric guitar for a while so I could learn it and make some music)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So in conclusion: &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am grateful to make such a heavy progress in the game. Patience, discipline and determination will help me a lot in life.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A familiar failure: the stage hits 99% and stalls. 199 tasks are done, one is still running, and adding nodes doesn't help, because the bottleneck is one partition, not the cluster.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's &lt;strong&gt;data skew&lt;/strong&gt;. In this post I'll go through how I diagnose it and how I fix it on Azure Databricks as of 2026, roughly in the order I'd try each fix. Every snippet runs on Databricks Free Edition, which is serverless-only, so I'll flag the places where serverless behaves differently.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Not affiliated with or endorsed by Databricks. Config names and defaults are from the Apache Spark and Databricks docs as of October 2026. Check them against your runtime version.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What skew actually is
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A shuffle sends every row with the same key to the same partition. That's what makes joins and &lt;code&gt;groupBy&lt;/code&gt; work. If one key holds 30% of your rows, one task gets 30% of the work, and the stage waits for it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual culprits:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;NULL&lt;/code&gt; or placeholder keys (&lt;code&gt;'UNKNOWN'&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;-1&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;''&lt;/code&gt;). In my experience this is the cause more often than anything else.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A handful of "whale" entities: one huge customer, one default store, one bot user agent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Time skew: one day (Black Friday, a backfill date) is 50Ã— the others.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;File/read skew&lt;/strong&gt;, which isn't about keys at all. A few huge or unsplittable files (gzipped CSV, for example) make the &lt;em&gt;first&lt;/em&gt; stage lopsided.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 1: Build a skewed dataset to practice on
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Paste this into a Free Edition notebook. It creates 20M orders: ~30% with a &lt;code&gt;NULL&lt;/code&gt; customer, ~20% for one whale customer (&lt;code&gt;42&lt;/code&gt;):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kn"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;pyspark.sql&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kn"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;functions&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;N&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20_000_000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;spark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;N&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;7&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;None&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bigint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ~30% NULL
&lt;/span&gt;         &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;42&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bigint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# ~20% whale
&lt;/span&gt;         &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;otherwise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;11&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1_000_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;bigint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;amount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;drop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;spark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1_000_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumnRenamed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;segment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;enterprise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;otherwise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;smb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Step 2: Confirm it. Don't guess.
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key distribution.&lt;/strong&gt; This is the cheapest check and the one I always run first:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;groupBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pct_of_rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;round&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;orderBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;desc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;show&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Double-digit percentages at the top mean you've found it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Partition-level distribution.&lt;/strong&gt; This shows what a shuffle on that key does to your tasks:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;repartition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;groupBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;spark_partition_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;pid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;agg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;max_rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;percentile_approx&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;median_rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;max_over_median&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;max_rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;/&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;median_rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;show&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A max/median ratio in the tens or hundreds means one task will do most of the work, no matter how big your cluster is.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In the Spark UI (classic compute).&lt;/strong&gt; Open the slow stage and look at &lt;strong&gt;Summary Metrics&lt;/strong&gt;. Compare &lt;em&gt;Max&lt;/em&gt; with &lt;em&gt;Median&lt;/em&gt; for Duration, Shuffle Read Size / Records, and Spill (Disk). A healthy stage has a fairly flat distribution. A skewed stage has a median of seconds and a max of minutes, and the Event Timeline shows one long bar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;On serverless (including Free Edition)&lt;/strong&gt; there's no Spark UI. Click &lt;strong&gt;See performance&lt;/strong&gt; under the cell and open the &lt;strong&gt;query profile&lt;/strong&gt;. Look for the operator with a disproportionate share of time and rows. It's operator-level, not a per-task histogram, so the key-distribution query above is even more useful there.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 3: Let AQE do its job (and know when it can't)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Adaptive Query Execution re-plans at stage boundaries using real shuffle statistics. It's on by default in Spark 3.2+ and on Databricks. Its skew-join feature splits an oversized partition into smaller ones and replicates the matching partition on the other side. A partition counts as skewed only when &lt;strong&gt;both&lt;/strong&gt; of these are true:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;it's larger than &lt;code&gt;spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.skewedPartitionFactor&lt;/code&gt; (default &lt;strong&gt;5&lt;/strong&gt;) Ã— the median partition size, &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;it's larger than &lt;code&gt;spark.sql.adaptive.skewJoin.skewedPartitionThresholdInBytes&lt;/code&gt; (default &lt;strong&gt;256MB&lt;/strong&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Split sizes aim at &lt;code&gt;spark.sql.adaptive.advisoryPartitionSizeInBytes&lt;/code&gt; (default &lt;strong&gt;64MB&lt;/strong&gt;). When it works, the final plan shows a &lt;code&gt;SortMergeJoin&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;isSkew=true&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;joined&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;joined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;overwrite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;noop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;save&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# run it without writing anything
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;noop&lt;/code&gt; runs the whole job without writing output. Then check the plan in the query profile (serverless) or the SQL tab (classic).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What AQE &lt;strong&gt;won't&lt;/strong&gt; handle, according to the docs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Broadcast joins.&lt;/strong&gt; Skew handling only applies to sort-merge and shuffle-hash joins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Some join sides.&lt;/strong&gt; In a &lt;code&gt;LEFT OUTER&lt;/code&gt; join only the left side can be split. &lt;code&gt;FULL OUTER&lt;/code&gt; gets no skew handling at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Partitions under 256MB.&lt;/strong&gt; If your "skew" is 200MB against a 2MB median, the threshold blocks it. On classic compute you can lower the thresholds. &lt;strong&gt;On serverless you can't&lt;/strong&gt;, because only a short allowlist of Spark confs (like &lt;code&gt;spark.sql.shuffle.partitions&lt;/code&gt;, which defaults to &lt;code&gt;auto&lt;/code&gt; there) can be set.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scan-stage skew, aggregations and window functions.&lt;/strong&gt; The skew-join rule is about joins.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One honest caveat about the demo: on a small Free Edition dataset the skewed partition may not cross 256MB, so you may not see &lt;code&gt;isSkew=true&lt;/code&gt;. That's the threshold working as designed, not a broken demo.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 4: Broadcast the small side
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the other side fits in memory, a broadcast hash join removes the shuffle completely. With no shuffle there are no hot partitions, so skew stops mattering:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;joined&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;broadcast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Spark auto-broadcasts below &lt;code&gt;spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold&lt;/code&gt; (10MB in OSS). Databricks AQE can also switch to a broadcast at runtime below &lt;code&gt;spark.databricks.adaptive.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold&lt;/code&gt; (documented at 30MB). Databricks' own guidance is still to hint the broadcast when you know a side is small, because a planned broadcast beats one that AQE only chooses after both sides have already shuffled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two cautions: compressed Parquet can expand a lot in memory, and you can't broadcast the preserved side of an outer join (the left side of a &lt;code&gt;LEFT JOIN&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 5: Treat hot keys separately (especially NULLs)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For &lt;code&gt;NULL&lt;/code&gt; keys this is free. NULLs never match in an equi-join, so pushing them through the shuffle is pure waste:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;is_null&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isNull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;matched&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;~&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;is_null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;nulls&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;is_null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;            &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# no partner rows to find anyway
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;matched&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;unionByName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;nulls&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;allowMissingColumns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="bp"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For an inner join, just filter the NULLs out. The same split works for a known whale key: send it through a broadcast path and the rest through the normal join.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 6: Salting, the targeted version
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Salting spreads one key across &lt;em&gt;N&lt;/em&gt; partitions by adding a random suffix on the big side and replicating the small side &lt;em&gt;N&lt;/em&gt; times. The common version salts &lt;em&gt;every&lt;/em&gt; key, which multiplies the whole dimension table by &lt;em&gt;N&lt;/em&gt;. I prefer to salt &lt;strong&gt;only the hot keys&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;SALT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;16&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;hot_keys&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;42&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# take these from your Step 2 profiling, not from a hard-coded guess
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;orders_s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hot_keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;SALT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;otherwise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;customers_s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;customers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;explode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;when&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;col&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hot_keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sequence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;SALT&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;otherwise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;lit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;salted&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;orders_s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;customers_s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;left&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;drop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Only the whale's dimension row gets replicated (16 rows, not 16 million). Size &lt;code&gt;SALT&lt;/code&gt; so each slice of the hot key is close to a normal partition.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For aggregations, be selective too.&lt;/strong&gt; Spark already does partial aggregation on the map side, so plain &lt;code&gt;sum&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; on a skewed key often hurts less than you'd expect. Salting pays off for aggregations that can't be combined that way, like &lt;code&gt;collect_list&lt;/code&gt;, exact &lt;code&gt;countDistinct&lt;/code&gt; and percentiles, and for window functions partitioned by a hot key. Two-phase aggregation looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;partial&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;withColumn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;rand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;seed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;SALT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;groupBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;agg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;amount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)))&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;final&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;partial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;groupBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;customer_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;agg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;total_amount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;F&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;alias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;order_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Step 7: Repartitioning (and its trap)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;repartition(400, "customer_id")&lt;/code&gt; does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; fix key skew. Every row for key 42 still hashes to the same partition, and you've paid for a shuffle. What helps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;repartition(400)&lt;/code&gt; with no key (round-robin) when you just need even work for narrow, row-by-row processing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;code&gt;REBALANCE&lt;/code&gt; hint before a write. AQE can split skewed partitions in a rebalance (&lt;code&gt;spark.sql.adaptive.optimizeSkewsInRebalancePartitions.enabled&lt;/code&gt;, default &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt;), which avoids one giant output file:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;orders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;hint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;rebalance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;mode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;overwrite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;saveAsTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;workspace.default.orders_demo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;(&lt;code&gt;workspace.default&lt;/code&gt; is the default catalog and schema on Free Edition. Use your own elsewhere.)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Step 8: Fix the layout when the skew is on read
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the &lt;em&gt;first&lt;/em&gt; stage is the lopsided one, AQE can't help, because there's no shuffle statistic to re-plan from yet. That's a file-layout problem: a few giant files, thousands of tiny ones, unsplittable compression, or Hive-style partitions where one &lt;code&gt;date=&lt;/code&gt; folder is huge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Delta, the current recommendation for new tables is &lt;strong&gt;liquid clustering&lt;/strong&gt;. Databricks lists "tables with heavy data skew" among its target use cases:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;spark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE workspace.default.orders_clustered
CLUSTER BY (customer_id)
AS SELECT * FROM workspace.default.orders_demo
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;spark&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sql&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;OPTIMIZE workspace.default.orders_clustered&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sh"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Be clear about what this buys you: evenly sized files and better data skipping. It does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; remove skew from a later join on &lt;code&gt;customer_id&lt;/code&gt;. The shuffle still sends key 42 to one partition. With predictive optimization on Unity Catalog managed tables, &lt;code&gt;CLUSTER BY AUTO&lt;/code&gt; lets Databricks choose the keys.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How to explain this in an interview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Interviewers are checking whether you have a &lt;em&gt;process&lt;/em&gt;, not a definition. A structure that works:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Symptom:&lt;/strong&gt; "A stage stuck at 99% with one long task. Max task duration far above the median, a big shuffle read on that task, often spill."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Confirm:&lt;/strong&gt; "I profile the key distribution, and the first thing I check is NULL and default keys."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fix in order of cost:&lt;/strong&gt; "Check that AQE skew join is applicable (join type, thresholds), broadcast the small side if I can, route NULL or hot keys around the join, and use targeted salting as a last resort."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Know the limits:&lt;/strong&gt; "AQE only handles skew in shuffle joins. It won't touch broadcast joins or scan-stage skew, and on serverless I can't tune its thresholds."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Close the loop:&lt;/strong&gt; "Then I verify. I check for &lt;code&gt;isSkew=true&lt;/code&gt; in the plan, compare max against median again, and add a skew check to the pipeline so a new whale customer doesn't bring it back."&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Point 5 is where you stand out. Most candidates stop at "I'd use salting."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Max vs. median task duration and shuffle read (Spark UI), or the slowest operator (query profile)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Top-10 key distribution, including &lt;code&gt;NULL&lt;/code&gt; and placeholder values&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Is it a join, an aggregation, a window function, or the scan stage?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] AQE on and applicable: shuffle join, a join type that allows splitting that side, partitions over the thresholds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Small side broadcastable? Hint it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] NULLs filtered or routed around the join&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Salt only the hot keys, sized from real numbers&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Scan-stage skew â†’ fix the files (compaction, liquid clustering), not the join&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Re-measure, then add monitoring&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;p&gt;If you're prepping for Databricks interviews, I put 10 questions like this with model answers into a free sample: &lt;a href="https://pulyala.gumroad.com/l/vonemy" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://pulyala.gumroad.com/l/vonemy&lt;/a&gt; (the full 61-question pack is here: &lt;a href="https://pulyala.gumroad.com/l/ivjsaf" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://pulyala.gumroad.com/l/ivjsaf&lt;/a&gt;).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Not affiliated with Databricks. Databricks and Azure Databricks are trademarks of their respective owners.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


The Looming Cryptographic Cliff
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cryptocurrency and Web3 ecosystem is built on a single mathematical premise: &lt;strong&gt;Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECDSA secp256k1 and Ed25519)&lt;/strong&gt; guarantees that only the holder of a private key can authorize a transaction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That premise has a finite shelf life.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When &lt;strong&gt;Q-Day&lt;/strong&gt; arrives—the point where a fault-tolerant quantum computer scales to &lt;strong&gt;4,096+ Logical Qubits (FTQC)&lt;/strong&gt;—Shor’s Algorithm will break asymmetric key pairs in polynomial time (~8 hours).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is the chronological timeline of how the crypto apocalypse will unfold, the direct threat vectors, and the emergency survival steps required to protect decentralized infrastructure.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Chronological Timeline: The Road to Q-Day
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                             THE Q-DAY TIMELINE                                   │
├──────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Era              │ Key Milestone &amp;amp; Threat Vector                                 │
├──────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Today (Pre-Q-Day)│ "Harvest Now, Decrypt Later" (HNDL) data archiving active    │
│ Phase 1: Near-Term│ NISQ to FTQC scaling (1,024 → 2,048 logical qubits)          │
│ Phase 2: Q-Day    │ 4,096+ FTQC reached: RSA-2048 and ECDSA broken in ~8 hours    │
│ Phase 3: Post-Q  │ PQC Migration Window closes; non-agile chains insolvents      │
└──────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


1. Today (The HNDL Phase)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Threat actors and state agencies are actively intercepting and storing encrypted network traffic and public blockchain transactions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Public keys broadcast during past transactions are indexed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Encrypted P2P node communications (TLS/Noise) are archived for retroactive decryption.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Phase 1: The Quantum Scaling Horizon
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As quantum hardware moves from Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices to Fault-Tolerant Quantum Computing (FTQC):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;2,048 Logical Qubits:&lt;/strong&gt; RSA-1024 breaks. Initial panic hits legacy banking infrastructure.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;4,096+ Logical Qubits (Q-Day Threshold):&lt;/strong&gt; RSA-2048, ECDSA P-256, and secp256k1 are completely broken.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


How the Apocalypse Hits Web3: Vector Analysis
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Layer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Vulnerable Standard&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Failure Mode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Impact&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Address/UTXO Layer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ECDSA / secp256k1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Private key derivation from exposed public keys&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;6.5M+ BTC&lt;/strong&gt; drained or frozen&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Smart Contract Layer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ECDSA Signatures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Spoofed multi-sig &amp;amp; oracle signatures&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Total loss of protocol TVL&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Network Layer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Classical TLS / Noise&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) &amp;amp; Eclipse attacks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Peer-to-Peer network isolation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Consensus Layer&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SHA-256 / Keccak-256&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Quadratic speedup via Grover's Algorithm&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lowered cost of 51% attacks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The "Freeze or Steal" Governance Crisis
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For legacy blockchains like Bitcoin, responding to Q-Day presents a brutal protocol dilemma:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Option A (No Action):&lt;/strong&gt; Quantum actors derive private keys from exposed public key addresses (P2PK and reused UTXOs) and drain funds into liquid exchanges.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Option B (Emergency Hard Fork - e.g., BIP-361):&lt;/strong&gt; The protocol freezes or burns all un-migrated legacy outputs to protect remaining liquidity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Outcome:&lt;/strong&gt; In both scenarios, non-migrated wallet holders face a &lt;strong&gt;100% permanent loss&lt;/strong&gt; of their funds.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The Survival Playbook: Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC)
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To prevent total protocol insolvency, blockchain networks, wallet providers, and core developers must execute immediate PQC upgrades:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


For Blockchain Core Developers
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Implement NIST-Approved Signature Schemes:&lt;/strong&gt; Upgrade signature verification from ECDSA to &lt;strong&gt;ML-DSA (Dilithium)&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;SLH-DSA (SPHINCS+)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Account Abstraction (ERC-4337 / EIP-7702):&lt;/strong&gt; Decouple signature verification logic from protocol core so wallets can swap to quantum-resistant verification algorithms dynamically.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


For Crypto Holders &amp;amp; Developers
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Avoid Address Reuse:&lt;/strong&gt; Never reuse P2PKH addresses. Ensure public keys are only revealed during necessary outgoing transactions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Migrate to Quantum-Agile Wallets:&lt;/strong&gt; Move assets to smart accounts that support lattice-based key verification once deployed.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The approaching crypto apocalypse is not a software bug—it is an impending mathematical reality. Protocols that fail to implement &lt;strong&gt;Post-Quantum Cryptography&lt;/strong&gt; before the 4,096+ logical qubit threshold is crossed will face complete cryptographic insolvency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’ve been reviewing a lot of AI-generated code. Most of it runs. A lot of it still has the same junk: narration left in the file, markdown fences, “rest of the code…” comments, and constants that get renamed but keep the same bad values.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most tools don’t catch that last one. They look at names. Matrix Reader looks at the number. Same tainted magnitude under a new name still gets flagged.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It also catches the AI residue normal linters skip, understands C++ public/exported functions so it doesn’t spam false “unused” hits, and runs fully local with zero third-party deps.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built and run it from a phone in Termux. That’s why it stays light and portable — not locked to a desktop IDE or a cloud review bot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Commercial tool, not open-source freeware. For people who actually ship agent-written code and are tired of cleaning up after it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Why a resilient cross-platform layer reduces certification churn&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Designing the core service interfaces: &lt;code&gt;User&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Storage&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Achievements&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Networking&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Handling errors, sandboxing, and graceful fallbacks that survive certification&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Testing, CI integration, and API versioning strategies for console builds&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Practical application: checklists, interface stubs, and a CI pipeline recipe&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Platform differences are the single biggest schedule risk when shipping across PlayStation, Xbox, and Switch. Neglecting a tight cross-platform abstraction produces duplicated logic, subtle platform-specific bugs, and repeated certification failures.   &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The symptoms you feel every release — late-night platform-specific debugging, build permutations that fail only on cert, and feature toggles that leak into gameplay — come from the same root cause: a brittle or overreaching cross-platform layer. Certification gates (Sony’s TRC, Microsoft’s XRs, Nintendo’s Lotcheck) check platform-level behaviors such as save integrity, suspend/resume, and network error handling; failing any of those tests forces rework, resubmission, and schedule risk.     Performance tooling and platform-specific profilers exist, but they only help if your abstraction makes platform differences visible and testable rather than hidden and brittle.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why a resilient cross-platform layer reduces certification churn
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You want the rest of the game team to write engine and gameplay code without constantly thinking about whether the call will pass cert or crash a devkit. That means the cross-platform layer must be &lt;em&gt;predictable&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;testable&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;explicit about capabilities&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep the layer &lt;em&gt;thin and focused&lt;/em&gt;. Abstract surface area, not implementation: expose the behaviors the game needs, not the entire platform SDK. A thin facade prevents a single adapter change from cascading into the whole codebase.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Model capabilities, not features. Don’t pretend every platform supports identical semantics for achievements, cloud saves, or matchmaking — expose a &lt;code&gt;PlatformCaps&lt;/code&gt; bitfield so higher-level code queries features at runtime.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make platform failures visible but safe. Map platform SDK errors to a small set of &lt;em&gt;domain&lt;/em&gt; error categories (&lt;code&gt;NotSignedIn&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Network&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;StorageFull&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;PolicyError&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Transient&lt;/code&gt;) and treat them uniformly in game code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Design for certification items as first-class API contracts. Treat TRC/XR/Lotcheck requirements (suspend/resume, atomic saves, controller disconnect behavior) as non-functional acceptance tests in your API contract, and put checks in CI.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Important:&lt;/strong&gt; Certification is not a QA afterthought — it's part of your API contract. Build your abstraction so the contract explicitly covers the behaviors the platform testers validate.   &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Platform differences at a glance&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Platform&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cert Name&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;SDK access&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cloud saves&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Achievements&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Profilers&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Typical gotcha&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;PlayStation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TRC / Technical Requirements Checklist&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Partner portal / NDA required.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Title-dependent (partner docs).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trophies (integrated via PSN; partner docs).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;Razor&lt;/code&gt; referenced in engine docs.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;TRC rules are strict about suspend/resume and save integrity.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Xbox&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;XRs / Xbox Requirements (XR)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Xbox GDK; public docs and ID@Xbox onboarding.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloud saves supported; integrated with Xbox services.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Achievements via Xbox Services API; Achievements Manager API and offline queue semantics exist.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;PIX&lt;/code&gt; for deep CPU/GPU captures.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Submission Validator and XR test cases run during certification.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Nintendo Switch&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lotcheck / Lotcheck certification&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Developer Portal gating and approval.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Save Data Cloud feature depends on title and Nintendo Online rules.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No universal trophy system; platform feature set differs.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Platform-specific tools; memory constraints are common.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Limited memory and Lotcheck timing make save handling and performance critical.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sources for the facts in the table are listed at the end of the article.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Designing the core service interfaces: &lt;code&gt;User&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Storage&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Achievements&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Networking&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Design each core service as a small, well-documented interface that answers a single question. Use C++-style interface examples as the lingua franca in cross-studio code, but the shape applies to any language.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Principles&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Prefer behavior-based names: &lt;code&gt;SignInAsync&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;SaveAtomic&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;QueueAchievement&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;SendReliable&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make methods asynchronous where I/O or platform UI is involved.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Return a platform-agnostic &lt;code&gt;Result&amp;lt;T, PlatformError&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;Expected&amp;lt;T,Error&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;) so calling code can retry, show friendly UI, or degrade.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Provide a capability query: &lt;code&gt;PlatformCaps GetCapabilities()&lt;/code&gt; that your UI/UX and systems can read at startup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example interface stubs (illustrative; adapt to your engine conventions):&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cpp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// PlatformAbstraction.h&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cp"&gt;#pragma once
#include&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cpf"&gt;&amp;lt;string&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;
#include&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cpf"&gt;&amp;lt;future&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;
#include&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cpf"&gt;&amp;lt;vector&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;
#include&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cpf"&gt;&amp;lt;cstdint&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cp"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;enum&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;NotSignedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;NetworkUnavailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;StorageFull&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;PermissionDenied&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Transient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;Unknown&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;UserInfo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;platformId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;       &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// XUID / NP Account ID / Nintendo Account ID (opaque)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;displayName&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;isSignedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IPlatformUser&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;public:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;~&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;IPlatformUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;UserInfo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;SignInAsync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;UserInfo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;GetLocalUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;IsSignedIn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IPlatformStorage&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;public:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;~&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;IPlatformStorage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;SaveAtomic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;uint8_t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pair&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;uint8_t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Load&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;HasCloudSave&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IPlatformAchievements&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;public:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;~&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;IPlatformAchievements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;QueueUnlock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;achievementId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FlushQueue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// attempts to sync queued unlocks&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;IPlatformNetworking&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;public:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;~&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;IPlatformNetworking&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;IsNetworkAvailable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;virtual&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;future&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ResolveMatchmakingTicket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ticket&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Notes:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expose &lt;em&gt;opaque&lt;/em&gt; platform IDs to avoid leaking platform-specific formatting into gameplay code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Achievements should expose a queue API so unlocking can occur offline and sync later; Xbox’s Achievements Manager documentation describes client-side sync semantics and managers for keeping state current. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Adapter pattern, not a big SDK wrapper
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Implement per-platform adapters (&lt;code&gt;PlatformAdapter_Xbox&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;PlatformAdapter_PS&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;PlatformAdapter_Switch&lt;/code&gt;) that implement the above interfaces. The adapter should be a thin translator between your domain model and the console SDK. Keep the mapping code localized so changes in a platform SDK only affect one file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Handling errors, sandboxing, and graceful fallbacks that survive certification
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A robust cross-platform layer makes failures manageable and predictable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Error mapping and handling&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Map vendor errors to &lt;code&gt;PlatformError&lt;/code&gt; as early as possible; never leak raw HRESULTs or platform exceptions beyond the adapter boundary.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For &lt;em&gt;transient&lt;/em&gt; errors (network hiccups, service throttling), use an idempotent retry with exponential backoff + jitter. For &lt;em&gt;permanent&lt;/em&gt; errors (permission denied), fall back immediately to a degraded UX.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Log platform raw errors (with a controlled scrubbed telemetry channel) so you can correlate cert failures to the specific platform code path and stack trace.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sandboxing platform calls&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run platform SDK calls that may block or open system UI on dedicated worker threads or in an isolated helper process. Don't call platform sign-in or file-system sync on the render or main gameplay thread.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Wrap calls in a watchdog with timeouts to prevent certification failures caused by deadlock or long blocking operations (the platform cert testers check for responsiveness). &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Atomic save example (pattern — platform-specific sync is required)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cpp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;SaveAtomic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;uint8_t&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Write to temp file&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tmp&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;".tmp"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ofstream&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tmp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;binary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;reinterpret_cast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;char&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;());&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;flush&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Ensure OS-level flush (platform-specific): call fsync on file descriptor here.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Atomically rename the temp file to final path&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;filesystem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;rename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;tmp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Use the platform-recommended flush &amp;amp; rename semantics — they are often the difference between a TRC pass and a fail.  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Graceful fallbacks&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Feature gating: at runtime, if &lt;code&gt;GetCapabilities()&lt;/code&gt; shows &lt;code&gt;Caps_CloudSaves == false&lt;/code&gt;, the UI should expose only local save flows and disable cloud-specific UIs.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Queue-and-sync: achievements and telemetry should be queued locally and uploaded when connectivity or services are available; Xbox documentation shows title-managed achievements and offline sync behavior models you can emulate. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Policy &amp;amp; privacy: implement a policy adapter that maps platform consent settings and parental controls into a single &lt;code&gt;UserPolicy&lt;/code&gt; object your gameplay systems read.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Testing, CI integration, and API versioning strategies for console builds
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Testing and CI are where your abstraction proves its worth.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CI and pre-cert automation&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build matrix: host (editor/dev), Xbox GDK build, PlayStation build, Switch build. Automate artifacts and label them with adapter and SDK versions (see versioning below).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run unit tests and engine regression tests on host builds; run targeted integration smoke tests on devkits for platform-specific behavior (sign-in, suspend/resume, saves).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use platform tooling as part of CI: Xbox Submission Validator / &lt;code&gt;MakePkg.exe&lt;/code&gt; and automated checks should be part of your pipeline before you submit to cert, reducing back-and-forth. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automate performance capture where possible: PIX offers command-line tooling and timing capture automation which you can schedule in nightly runs to catch regressions. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;API versioning strategy&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;em&gt;semantic versioning&lt;/em&gt; for your cross-platform adapter libraries and internal SDK wrappers. Mark breaking changes with a major adapter version bump and keep the adapter version visible in your build metadata. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Version your adapter separately from the game build. Example: &lt;code&gt;game v1.3.0 + xbox-adapter v2.0.0&lt;/code&gt;. That separation lets you roll out adapter patches independently for hotfixes and cert revalidation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;For runtime compatibility, include a &lt;code&gt;platform_manifest.json&lt;/code&gt; embedded in each build that declares &lt;code&gt;adapter_version&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;sdk_build&lt;/code&gt;, and &lt;code&gt;capabilities&lt;/code&gt;. The game can assert compatibility at startup and produce a human-readable diagnostic if a mismatch is detected.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Example platform manifest&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"platform"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"xbox"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"adapter_version"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"2.1.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"sdk_build"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"GDK-16.0"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"capabilities"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"achievements"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cloud_saves"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"rich_presence"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Testing recommendations (practical)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Unit-test adapters by mocking vendor SDK calls (wrap vendor calls behind a thin wrapper interface you can mock).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run nightly device tests: a small suite that covers suspend/resume, sign-in/out, save/load, achievements queue flush, and a Smoke VR/Audio test if applicable.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automate Submission Validator and include its exit codes in the CI job so you only upload builds that pass the initial artifact checks. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Automate headless PIX captures (or platform profiler equivalents) to detect CPU/GPU regressions. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Practical application: checklists, interface stubs, and a CI pipeline recipe
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Checklist — architecture &amp;amp; implementation&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Define &lt;code&gt;IPlatformUser&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;IPlatformStorage&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;IPlatformAchievements&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;IPlatformNetworking&lt;/code&gt; contracts and document TRC/XR behaviors they must satisfy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Implement &lt;code&gt;PlatformCaps&lt;/code&gt; and surface it at startup.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Create per-platform adapters with a single factory: &lt;code&gt;Platform::CreateAdapter(PlatformId)&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Implement local queues for achievements and telemetry; implement &lt;code&gt;FlushQueue()&lt;/code&gt; invoked on network restoration or explicit user sign-in.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Implement &lt;code&gt;SaveAtomic()&lt;/code&gt; and on startup validate save integrity; include a user-visible recovery flow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Add adapter and SDK versioning to build metadata and publish manifest with builds.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;[ ] Integrate Submission Validator / packaging into CI (packaging + pre-cert checks). &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Quick adapter factory pattern (sketch)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cpp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;unique_ptr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;IPlatformAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;CreateAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;switch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Xbox&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;make_unique&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;XboxAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;PlayStation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;make_unique&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;PlayStationAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Switch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;make_unique&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;SwitchAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;default:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;make_unique&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;NullAdapter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// for tools, editor&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;CI pipeline recipe (pseudo-YAML)&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;stages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;build&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;host-build&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;xbox-build&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;ps5-build&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;switch-build&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;test&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;unit-tests&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;integration-smoke (runs on devkit farm)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;pre-cert&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;submission-validator (MakePkg.exe / Submission Validator for Xbox)&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# fail-fast&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;performance-diff (pixtool timing captures)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;jobs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="pi"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;create-submission-package&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="pi"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;sign-and-upload-to-sandbox&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Notes: make the &lt;code&gt;integration-smoke&lt;/code&gt; stage run on reserved devkits with environment isolation. Use per-platform feature flags to toggle heavy tests during a hotfix cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pre-cert checklist (quick)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build a &lt;em&gt;clean&lt;/em&gt; release build with the production configuration and packaging. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run Submission Validator / sandbox download test. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run the smoke suite on each devkit: sign-in, save, load, achievement unlock + queue flush, suspend/resume, controller disconnect/reconnect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run designated profiler captures (PIX/Razor) and ensure no heavy regressions in CPU/GPU budgets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Confirm manifest &lt;code&gt;adapter_version&lt;/code&gt; matches supported adapter list and document any breaking adapter changes in release notes. &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sample achievement queue pseudocode&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight cpp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;AchievementQueue&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;queue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;IPlatformAchievements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;public:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Enqueue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;std&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;PersistQueueToLocalStorage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Flush&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;while&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;empty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;auto&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;front&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;auto&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;api&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;QueueUnlock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;PersistQueueToLocalStorage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;continue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;err&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Transient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Transient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// try later&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// for permanent errors, drop or log per policy&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;pop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;PlatformError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On platform sign-in or network restoration call &lt;code&gt;Flush()&lt;/code&gt; on a worker thread.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Closing paragraph (no header)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Designing a robust platform SDK abstraction is less about hiding every vendor detail and more about &lt;em&gt;making platform differences first-class, testable, and constrained&lt;/em&gt; so they stop surprising you during certification; version your adapters, run pre-cert checks in CI, and treat TRC/XR/Lotcheck behaviors as contract items rather than optional work.    &lt;/p&gt;

