&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;↑ Back to contents&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Safari thought it was a card form
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Safari decides a form is a payment form from its field names and labels. A field called &lt;strong&gt;Payment&lt;/strong&gt; next to an amount was enough. The fix:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;NO_AUTOFILL&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;autoComplete&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;off&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;data-1p-ignore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;data-lpignore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That goes on every input, plus &lt;code&gt;autoComplete="off"&lt;/code&gt; on the form and neutral &lt;code&gt;name&lt;/code&gt;s (&lt;code&gt;paidWith&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;spent&lt;/code&gt;). The label still says "Payment". Only the attributes Safari reads changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;↑ Back to contents&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


The keyboard ate the sheet
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the iOS keyboard opens, the layout viewport doesn't shrink. Safari scrolls the &lt;em&gt;visual&lt;/em&gt; viewport instead, so a &lt;code&gt;position: fixed&lt;/code&gt; sheet ends up partly behind the keyboard and partly above the top of the screen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;visualViewport&lt;/code&gt; API reports both edges:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;top&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;vv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;offsetTop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;bottom&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;window&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;innerHeight&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;vv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;vv&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;offsetTop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fn6o4ogwlx3wpt911twpo.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fn6o4ogwlx3wpt911twpo.png" alt="User flow to add a Place" width="800" height="412"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The layout viewport never changes, so CSS alone can't see the keyboard. The visual viewport can.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The sheet pins itself between them through two CSS variables (&lt;code&gt;top-(--kb-top) bottom-(--kb)&lt;/code&gt; in Tailwind 4). It has a fixed header, a scrolling body and a fixed footer, so ✕ and &lt;strong&gt;Add place&lt;/strong&gt; stay in view while you type. Android gets the same result from &lt;code&gt;interactiveWidget: 'resizes-content'&lt;/code&gt; in the Next.js viewport.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;So what:&lt;/strong&gt; I had first fixed only the bottom inset. The header still vanished, because iOS scrolls the visible area up as well. One iPhone check caught it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;↑ Back to contents&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


A comma that emptied the field
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Depending on the phone's region settings, the decimal key on the iPhone keyboard types a comma. A &lt;code&gt;type="number"&lt;/code&gt; input given &lt;code&gt;4,50&lt;/code&gt; reports an empty value, so the amount silently disappeared.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Input&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"text"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;inputMode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"decimal"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The parser accepts both &lt;code&gt;,&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;.&lt;/code&gt;. You still get the numeric keyboard, and the browser no longer decides what a number looks like.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;↑ Back to contents&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


A pinch that started in the wrong place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;iOS Safari ignores &lt;code&gt;user-scalable=no&lt;/code&gt;. Cancelling &lt;code&gt;gesturestart&lt;/code&gt; stopped most page pinches, but not one that started on the timeline below the map. iOS makes a touch's moves uncancelable once it has begun as a scroll.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;documentElement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;style&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;touchAction&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;pan-x pan-y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That tells the browser never to start a page pinch, while one finger still scrolls. The map handles its own touches, so pinching it still zooms the map.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;↑ Back to contents&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Fewer taps, not faster typing
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The P1 fixes were about not typing at all:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fimkd0g0jkzoivyxfr9wr.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fimkd0g0jkzoivyxfr9wr.png" alt="Before and After comparison" width="799" height="288"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Typing became tapping, and the hunt for the new pin went away.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Place chip.&lt;/strong&gt; After a search, the place shows as name and address with a &lt;strong&gt;Change&lt;/strong&gt; button. Places API (New) &lt;code&gt;shortFormattedAddress&lt;/code&gt; is an Essentials field, so it adds no cost.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Tap-to-pick chips.&lt;/strong&gt; Category and payment are chips built from past entries, and payment starts on the one used last.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Notes that grow&lt;/strong&gt;, with &lt;code&gt;field-sizing: content&lt;/code&gt; and a fallback.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Fly to the new pin.&lt;/strong&gt; After saving, a toast appears, the map pans to the pin with its popup open, and the new timeline row rings briefly.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Afterwards I added one feature that wasn't in the brief: a full screen map where one finger pans. Google's own full screen button needs the Fullscreen API, which iPhone Safari doesn't have for page elements, so it's a fixed layer instead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;↑ Back to contents&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Try it yourself
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Record a real person doing the one thing your app is for, on their own phone, and time it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write the time down as the acceptance criterion, not "make it smoother".&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Check every iOS fix on a device after deploy, after a full reload.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The code is open source: &lt;a href="https://github.com/lfariabr/luisfaria.dev" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/lfariabr/luisfaria.dev&lt;/a&gt;. The epic is &lt;a href="https://github.com/lfariabr/luisfaria.dev/issues/388" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;#388&lt;/a&gt;, and there's a &lt;a href="https://github.com/lfariabr/luisfaria.dev/blob/master/_docs/guides/run-your-own-places-map.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guide to run your own Places map&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;React, deeply&lt;/strong&gt; · Chapter 1 of 12 · Web · new chapter every Monday night&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;By the end of this chapter:&lt;/strong&gt; Describe the render, reconcile and commit phases precisely enough to predict behaviour.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Emma is a front‑end engineer on a two‑month sprint for a real‑time analytics dashboard. The UI pulls live metrics from a WebSocket and updates a table every second. After merging a new “filter by region” feature, the dashboard starts missing updates: the table lags by up to three seconds, the CPU on the user’s laptop spikes to 90 % and the performance monitoring alert fires every time a user opens the page. The sprint deadline is three days away, and the team cannot afford to roll back the feature.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Emma knows that React’s &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt; call in the filter component is being invoked correctly – the state value changes, the UI shows the new region label – but she cannot see why the rest of the component tree is taking so long to reflect the new data. She has the React docs open, but they only describe the high‑level “React re‑renders when state changes”. She needs a concrete, step‑by‑step mental model of what React actually does after a state change, how the three phases (render, reconciliation, commit) interact, and how to predict whether a change will cause extra work. Only then can she pinpoint the bottleneck and fix the dashboard before the deadline.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Before you start
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Node&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;≥ 18.15.0
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;npm&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;≥ 9.6.0
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;React&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;18.2.0 and &lt;strong&gt;React‑DOM&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;18.2.0 (the current stable release as of October 2026)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A fresh create‑react‑app project (or any existing React 18 app) – run &lt;code&gt;npx create-react-app r18-demo --template cra-template-pwa&lt;/code&gt; to get a working baseline.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify the setup works by starting the dev server and seeing “Edit src/App.js” in the browser:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;cd &lt;/span&gt;r18-demo
npm start
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Expected output in the terminal:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# &amp;gt; Local:   http://localhost:3000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Open that URL – you should see the default CRA page.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the page renders, you have a correct environment and can proceed.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What does a state update trigger?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. Does React schedule work or run it immediately?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; (or the setter returned by &lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;) is called, React does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; synchronously touch the DOM. Instead it creates a &lt;em&gt;update&lt;/em&gt; object, pushes it onto the fiber’s update queue, and asks the scheduler to perform work. In React 18 the scheduler always &lt;strong&gt;batches&lt;/strong&gt; updates that happen within the same tick, even if they originate from different event sources.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Why it matters&lt;/em&gt;: If you call &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; inside a &lt;code&gt;setTimeout&lt;/code&gt;, React still batches that call with any other updates that happen before the next paint. Knowing the batching behaviour tells you whether a series of rapid state changes will cause a single render or many.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. Render phase – building the “virtual” tree
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During the &lt;strong&gt;render phase&lt;/strong&gt; React walks the fiber tree depth‑first, calling each component function to produce a new &lt;em&gt;React element&lt;/em&gt; tree (the “virtual DOM”). No side‑effects run here – even &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; callbacks are not executed. The result is a new set of fibers that mirror the element tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Key points:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Concept&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What happens&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it matters&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pure function execution&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Component bodies run with the current state/props.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guarantees determinism – the same inputs give the same output.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hooks order&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;React validates that the hook call order matches the previous render.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Mismatched order throws “Rendered more hooks than during the previous render”.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Early bail‑out&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;If a component’s props and state are shallow‑equal to the previous render, React may skip rendering its children (the &lt;em&gt;memo&lt;/em&gt; optimisation).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Prevents unnecessary work.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can confirm the render phase by adding &lt;code&gt;console.log('render', name)&lt;/code&gt; at the top of a component; you’ll see it run before any DOM changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. Reconciliation – diffing the old and new fibers
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After the new tree is built, React enters the &lt;strong&gt;reconciliation&lt;/strong&gt; (or “diff”) phase. It compares the new fibers with the previous ones to decide what needs to change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keyed children&lt;/strong&gt; – If a list has a stable &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; prop, React can match old and new items by key, moving existing DOM nodes instead of recreating them. Without keys, React falls back to index‑based matching, which can cause unnecessary re‑mounts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Type change&lt;/strong&gt; – If the element type changes (e.g., &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; → &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;span&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;), React discards the old fiber subtree and creates a new one.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Effect list construction&lt;/strong&gt; – While reconciling, React builds three linked lists: &lt;em&gt;Placement&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;Update&lt;/em&gt;, and &lt;em&gt;Deletion&lt;/em&gt;. These lists are later used in the commit phase.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cost of reconciliation is proportional to the number of fibers examined. A deep tree with many siblings can become expensive if keys are missing or if component props change on every render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


4. Commit phase – mutating the DOM and running effects
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;commit phase&lt;/strong&gt; is the only phase that mutates the host environment (the browser DOM). It proceeds in three sub‑steps:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Before mutation&lt;/strong&gt; – Runs &lt;code&gt;getSnapshotBeforeUpdate&lt;/code&gt; (class components) and prepares layout effects. No DOM changes yet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mutation&lt;/strong&gt; – Applies all changes from the effect lists: inserts, updates, deletions. This is where the browser repaints.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Layout effects&lt;/strong&gt; – Executes &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; callbacks synchronously after the DOM mutation but before the browser paints.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Passive effects&lt;/strong&gt; – Schedules &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; callbacks to run after the paint (asynchronously).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because layout effects run before the browser paints, they can block rendering if they perform heavy work. Passive effects, on the other hand, never block the paint.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


How to observe each phase in a real component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


1. How can I tell when the render phase starts and ends?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add a &lt;code&gt;console.time&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;console.timeEnd&lt;/code&gt; pair at the top of the component:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;TableRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;TableRow render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// component body …&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;timeEnd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;TableRow render&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/* … */&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;tr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The console will show a line like &lt;code&gt;TableRow render: 0.42ms&lt;/code&gt;. If you see many rows each taking &amp;gt; 1 ms, the render phase is a candidate for optimisation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


2. How do I see the reconciliation decisions?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React DevTools’ “Profiler” tab records &lt;em&gt;mount&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;update&lt;/em&gt; timings and shows a “flame graph”. Hover over a component to see whether the update was a “re‑render” (same type, same key) or a “re‑mount” (type changed, key missing). A re‑mount will display a “Mount” badge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


3. How can I verify when effects run relative to the DOM mutation?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Insert logs in both &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;layout effect – DOM ready&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;passive effect – after paint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Open the console and watch the order. Layout effects will always appear before the passive ones for the same update.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Build it – instrument a component to visualise the three phases
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is a minimal reproducible example that logs every phase for a component that displays a counter. Follow the steps exactly; each command’s output is shown so you can confirm you’re on track.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Create the component file&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="nb"&gt;mkdir&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-p&lt;/span&gt; src/diagnostics &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;touch &lt;/span&gt;src/diagnostics/PhaseLogger.jsx
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Add the code&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;src/diagnostics/PhaseLogger.jsx&lt;/code&gt;):
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;PhaseLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`render &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Render phase ends when the function returns&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;timeEnd&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`render &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;🔧 layout effect – count:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;🌀 passive effect – count:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Count: &lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;onClick&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;+1&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;button&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Why&lt;/em&gt;: &lt;code&gt;console.time&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;console.timeEnd&lt;/code&gt; surround the component body, marking the render phase. The two effects log when they fire.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Render the component&lt;/strong&gt; – replace the default &lt;code&gt;App&lt;/code&gt; content with &lt;code&gt;PhaseLogger&lt;/code&gt;:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/App.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PhaseLogger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./diagnostics/PhaseLogger&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"App"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;PhaseLogger&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;App&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Run the dev server&lt;/strong&gt; (if not already running):
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   npm start
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Observe the console&lt;/strong&gt; – open Chrome DevTools, go to the Console tab, and click the “+1” button three times. You should see a repeating pattern:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   render 0: 0.12ms
🔧 layout effect – count: 0
🌀 passive effect – count: 0
render 1: 0.09ms
🔧 layout effect – count: 1
🌀 passive effect – count: 1
…
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Confirmation&lt;/em&gt;: The render log appears &lt;strong&gt;before&lt;/strong&gt; any effect logs, proving the order of phases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Add a heavy computation to the render&lt;/strong&gt; to see the impact:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Inside PhaseLogger, before the return:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;heavy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;heavy sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;heavy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Reload the page and click the button. The render time will jump (e.g., &lt;code&gt;render 2: 12.4ms&lt;/code&gt;). This demonstrates how expensive work in the render phase directly slows the UI.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Move the heavy work to a layout effect&lt;/strong&gt;:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;heavy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;layout heavy sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;heavy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Remove the computation from the render body. Now the render logs stay ~0.1 ms, while the layout effect logs the heavy sum. The UI feels snappier because the render phase is fast, but the layout effect blocks the paint – you’ll notice a brief freeze after each click.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Finally, move the work to a passive effect&lt;/strong&gt;:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;heavy&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;e5&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;b&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;log&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;passive heavy sum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;heavy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now the render is fast, the layout effect does nothing, and the heavy sum appears &lt;em&gt;after&lt;/em&gt; the paint. The UI feels responsive, but the heavy work still consumes CPU in the background.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You have built a minimal instrumented component that demonstrates the three phases, their ordering, and the performance impact of doing work in each phase.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


When this breaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Symptom (what you see)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Likely cause&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Fix&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;“Maximum update depth exceeded”&lt;/strong&gt; (stack overflow)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A state update inside a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;) that depends on the same state, causing an infinite loop.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ensure the effect’s dependency array does not include the state you set, or guard the update with a condition. Example: &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; { if (ready) setState(...); }, [ready]);&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rows in a list lose their input values after a filter change&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Missing &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; on list items, causing React to treat them as new mounts on each filter.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Add a stable &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; prop (e.g., &lt;code&gt;key={row.id}&lt;/code&gt;) to every element in the array map.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;UI freezes for ~200 ms after a state change, even though the component is tiny&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Heavy computation performed during the render phase (or inside a &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Move the work to a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; (passive) or, if it must be synchronous, memoise it with &lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt; and ensure the memoisation dependencies are correct.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; logs appear **after&lt;/strong&gt; the DOM is painted**&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Running the app in React 18’s concurrent mode with &lt;code&gt;StrictMode&lt;/code&gt; enabled, which intentionally runs layout effects twice in development.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Remember that the double‑run only happens in dev; it does not affect production. If the double execution is problematic, temporarily disable &lt;code&gt;StrictMode&lt;/code&gt; for that subtree.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Profiler shows “mount” instead of “update” for a component that should only re‑render&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The component’s &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; changed between renders, or its type changed (e.g., switching from &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;ComponentA&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;ComponentB&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keep the component type and key stable; if you need to swap implementations, wrap them in a parent that preserves the key.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each of these failures can be reproduced by the instrumented &lt;code&gt;PhaseLogger&lt;/code&gt; component: add a &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; inside an effect that depends on the same state to trigger the infinite loop; remove &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; from a list to see lost inputs; insert a heavy loop in the render body to cause a freeze.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


What it costs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Technique&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Trade‑off&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Running heavy work in the render phase&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Guarantees the DOM reflects the latest data &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; the paint, but each render blocks the main thread, leading to jank. Use only for cheap calculations.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Using &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; for synchronisation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provides a hook that runs before the paint, useful for measuring layout or synchronising scroll positions. However, it blocks the paint and can cause noticeable pauses on low‑end devices.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Moving work to &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; (passive)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Keeps the UI responsive because the effect runs after the paint. The downside is that any DOM measurement must be deferred, and the UI may briefly show an intermediate state.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Relying on key‑based reconciliation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Greatly reduces unnecessary re‑mounts, but requires that every item in a list has a stable, unique identifier. Generating keys on the fly (e.g., using &lt;code&gt;index&lt;/code&gt;) defeats the optimisation and can cause state loss.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Batching updates (default in React 18)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reduces the number of renders, but can hide bugs where you expect an immediate DOM update after a &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt;. If you need the update instantly, use &lt;code&gt;flushSync&lt;/code&gt; (cost: forces a synchronous render, defeating the batch).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding these costs lets you decide where to place work in the lifecycle and when to sacrifice immediacy for smoother frames.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


In the interview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Typical interview prompt&lt;/strong&gt;  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;“Walk me through what happens when you call &lt;code&gt;setState&lt;/code&gt; in a function component. Include the phases React goes through and why the order matters.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Weak answer&lt;/strong&gt; – “React re‑renders the component and updates the DOM.”&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Why it’s weak&lt;/em&gt;: It mentions only the render, omits reconciliation and commit, ignores effects, and gives no insight into performance implications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Strong answer&lt;/strong&gt; –  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scheduling&lt;/strong&gt; – React creates an update object, pushes it onto the fiber’s queue, and asks the scheduler to perform work; updates are batched.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Render phase&lt;/strong&gt; – React calls the component function, builds a new virtual tree, and validates hook order; no side‑effects run.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reconciliation&lt;/strong&gt; – The new tree is diffed against the previous one; keys guide matching, and three effect lists (Placement, Update, Deletion) are built.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Commit phase&lt;/strong&gt; – React first runs &lt;code&gt;getSnapshotBeforeUpdate&lt;/code&gt;/layout effects, then mutates the DOM according to the effect lists, then runs passive effects after the paint.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why order matters&lt;/strong&gt; – Doing heavy work in render or layout effect blocks the paint, causing jank; passive effects run asynchronously and cannot block the UI.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Senior‑level expectation&lt;/strong&gt; – In addition to the above, discuss:  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The role of the &lt;strong&gt;Fiber&lt;/strong&gt; data structure and how it enables interruption (concurrent mode).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How &lt;strong&gt;strict mode&lt;/strong&gt; in development double‑invokes render and layout effects, and why that matters for debugging.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;cost model&lt;/strong&gt;: O(N) traversal of the fiber tree, the impact of missing keys, and the memory overhead of keeping two trees simultaneously.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Director/Engineering‑manager angle&lt;/strong&gt; – The interviewer may ask: “How would you decide whether to optimise a component based on this model?” A good answer references measurable render times, the cost of extra DOM nodes, and the business impact (e.g., latency SLA).  &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Follow‑up that separates theory from practice&lt;/strong&gt; – “Can you give an example of a bug you encountered that stemmed from misunderstanding the commit phase?” A candidate who describes an infinite render loop caused by a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; that set state without a guard demonstrates real‑world experience.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


Your tasks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Log the three phases in an existing component&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;console.time&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;console.timeEnd&lt;/code&gt; around the component body, and &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;/&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; logs as shown in the “Build it” section.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Done&lt;/strong&gt; when you can see the render log, layout effect log, and passive effect log in the correct order for each state change.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Identify a needless re‑mount&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
In a list component that renders &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;input&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; fields, remove the &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; prop, type into an input, then trigger a state change that filters the list. Observe that the input loses its value.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Done&lt;/strong&gt; when you add a stable &lt;code&gt;key&lt;/code&gt; and the input retains its value across filters.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Create and fix an infinite render loop&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Add a &lt;code&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; setCount(c =&amp;gt; c + 1), [count]);&lt;/code&gt; to a component. Observe the “Maximum update depth exceeded” error. Then fix it by adding a guard (&lt;code&gt;if (count &amp;lt; 5) setCount(...)&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Done&lt;/strong&gt; when the component updates without crashing and the console no longer shows the error.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Measure the impact of moving work between phases&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Implement a 100 ms &lt;code&gt;for&lt;/code&gt;‑loop in the render body, then move it to a &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;, then to a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. Use the Chrome Performance tab to record each version and note the &lt;em&gt;CPU time&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;frame rate&lt;/em&gt; differences.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Done&lt;/strong&gt; when you can state the measured frame‑time for each version and explain why the passive effect version is the most performant for UI responsiveness.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Optimise a list with missing keys&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Take a component that renders 500 rows, each with a button that toggles a local &lt;code&gt;selected&lt;/code&gt; state. Remove the keys, observe that toggling one row causes many rows to re‑mount. Add stable keys and verify that only the clicked row re‑renders (use the React Profiler to confirm).&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Done&lt;/strong&gt; when the Profiler shows a single “Update” for the clicked row and no unnecessary mounts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;







&lt;h2&gt;


Your tasks this week
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do the exercises above before the next chapter. Reading a tutorial and doing&lt;br&gt;
one are different activities and only one of them changes what you can build.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Stuck on any of them?&lt;/strong&gt; Say so — describe what you tried and what happened:&lt;br&gt;
